 be sure everything's  fine is it wasting wine a crime  who gets to drink that wine at the end  of the night surprisingly when we come  back in the next morning that wine is  always gone the inn at little washington  is the heart and soul of this tiny  virginia town with fewer than 150  residents  o'connell first knew it as a gas station  on family trips  but he had a vision i just thought it  was the most wonderful thing of  getting away from all reality going out  in the country and  finding some elegant  wonderful dinner his parents didn't want  him to become a chef  but a trip to europe after college only  cemented his desire to cook  i thought to myself yes i must do this  whatever it takes his instructor julia  child's famed cookbook  mastering the art of french cooking my  rule was that i would make the recipe  three times just as it was written  only then would i allow myself to  improvise  and do it my way in 1978 patrick bought  that gas station  and he's been living his dream for more  than 40 years  the inn now includes more than 20  buildings on 25 acres  a garden and some animals  i left my sanitizer in the car when the  pandemic forced o'connell to close in  mid-march  his staff of 170 shrank to about a dozen  in recent weeks just three people worked  the kitchen preparing meals for  furloughed employees  and their families when they reopen  is a matter of life and death for the  community the food and lodging tax the  inn collects  supplies 90 percent of the town's budget  while we're closed the town has no  income  and that makes reopening even more  critical it does  it does because you're affecting not  just your own staff  but all the other businesses in town the  b b's the small shops and stores  but you're funding the water system the  sewage system and every all the other  infrastructure for a small town  it's a level of responsibility that most  restaurants  don't imagine how do you think this is  going to transform how americans eat out  i think there's going to be a renewed  appreciation for whatever a restaurant  has to offer  and people are pent up  they want to get out you know there's  going to be someone out there who sees  this and thinks  that seems a little opulent for the  times no no no  i think i think the problem is the  confusion  over whether or not we're still allowed  to have  fun and i think  the era that we've entered has confused  a lot of people  in that they don't know how to act they  don't know how to be  and they're fearful not only of what  they're dealing with but of how they'll  be judged  by others o'connell is eager for his end  to once again be a place to gather  even if we must maintain a certain  distance  someone said to me restaurants might be  the final frontier of human interaction  restaurants are sort of the last  bastions of trust  and they're kind of like a new religion  a sacred place because food can't lie  you know when you put something in your  mouth  if it's wonderful or it's not and  there's no no getting around that for  cbs this morning saturday  at o'keefe washington virginia  i so agree with everything he said i  cannot wait  to eat a big sumptuous meal i  i was worried that ed wasn't getting  getting his opportunity to like partake  that there wasn't the  i mean you have to when you do a dish  you must eat it's amazing  i didn't think about that idea that  people might be worried about spending  money  on a meal like that but if you're  looking for the excuse  that town 90 percent they need that  money  there's no doubt about it if that's the  reason that you need to to have  in order to go
 

